<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#telugu-language">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Telugu language</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Telugu is a Dravidian language of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the main language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> and one of the most widely spoken languages of India. It is written in its own script, derived from Brahmi, and its earliest inscriptions date from the 6th century. Its literature begins with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nannaya">Nannaya</a>'s translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> in the 11th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/telugu-language.jpg" alt="Telugu language" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C1MM · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Telugu_speaker_map_India.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
